        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.248,
            0.139,
            0.742,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Penicillin was discovered in 1928 by Alexander Fleming, a bacteriologist from London. While studying harmful bacteria, he noticed that a mould on a discarded petri dish stopped the bacteria from growing. He realized the mould released a substance that killed the bacteria. This chance discovery led to the discovery of penicillin, the first antibiotic used to treat bacterial infections."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.747,
            0.129,
            0.867,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.747,
                0.129,
                0.867,
                0.26
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.277,
            0.885,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Though antibiotics are effective in protecting us against bacterial infections and have saved millions of lives since their discovery, their indiscriminate use has led to a decline in their effectiveness (Fig. 3.5a). Nowadays, there are news headlines about "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "antibiotic resistance",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", a phenomenon where bacteria that were earlier killed by a given antibiotic are found to survive and multiply despite treatment with that antibiotic. This makes"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.422,
            0.903,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "HOW ANTIBIOTIC RESISTANCE SPREADS IN THE COMMUNITY?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
